; ---- EGG-2: the plinth the sunken television stands on ---------------------------------------
; Talon's addendum §6 asks for a television underwater. A swimming body is CLAMPED to
; WaterGeometry.SwimLineY (-1.83) by AvatarMotor.Step - there is no diving in this game - so a set
; standing on the bed at -3.27 would be a metre and a half below the deepest a player can ever
; reach: present, correct-looking, unreachable. The plinth lifts it until the walk-in trigger
; straddles the swim line. Its top face IS BubbleTestLayout.SunkenTvBaseY (-2.05) and GUARD-1's
; downward ray in BubbleTestSelfTest.CheckPropHeights measures the two against each other, so this
; box and that constant cannot drift apart silently.
;
; Buried to -4.60, which is 1.33 m under the bake's own bed at r = 10.65 (-3.27) and 0.79 m under
; its shallowest corner (-2.71 at r = 9.95), so no face of it hangs in the water. The cap is 0.15 m
; of the section's neutral stone over the column, so it reads as a thing somebody built rather
; than as a box.
;
; SHADER-2, 2026-09-04: THE COLUMN IS SOLID STONE (green_neutral), NOT lake_murk. It was authored
; with `lake_murk.tres` -- the LAKE SURFACE material -- as its material_override, to get a
; "murk-toned" column. That is a transparent water shader, and putting it on a solid box is what
; produced the boxy artifact Talon reported under the sunken television:
;
;   * `lake_murk.gdshader:212` is `render_mode blend_mix, depth_draw_opaque, cull_disabled`. On a
;     transparent material depth_draw_opaque writes NO depth, and cull_disabled draws both sides
;     of the box, so the box's own far faces are composited over its near faces unsorted.
;   * Those far faces are `!FRONT_FACING`, which takes the shader's "seen from underneath" branch
;     (`lake_murk.gdshader:1296-1302`): ALBEDO = deep_col (0.0115, 0.0225, 0.0195) at
;     under_alpha 0.94. A near-black, near-opaque, hard-edged rectangle.
;   * `n_world = normalize(vec3(slope.x, 1.0, slope.y))` (line 1103) forces every fragment's
;     normal straight UP, so the box's vertical faces are lit as if they were flat water.
;
; The murk this column WAS trying to have is the real murk: the lake surface composites over it,
; exactly as it does over the television itself, which is the read Talon likes. Nothing in
; `lake_murk.tres`, `lake_murk.gdshader` or `bubble_film.tres` was touched to fix this. If a
; darker column is ever wanted, change THIS material -- do not point a solid mesh at the water.
[sub_resource type="BoxMesh" id="BoxMesh_egg2_plinth"]
size = Vector3(1.4, 2.4, 1.4)

